// A test card for the pointer and the input gate, the way en/tutorial.oddlg
// is one for the markup. Every shape and both gate states appear here, so a
// change that breaks one shows up by opening this rather than by reading the
// drawing code.

:: anchor=left size=20 pad=26 enter=type speed=44 line=1.4

@Signals Officer
:: point=tab.economy gate=1
That one. §**The economy**§ is where the money comes from, and while I am
pointing at it, it is the only thing that opens.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=tab.research round=1
Research is next door. A ring rather than a box, because sometimes the thing
worth looking at is not a rectangle.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=label.treasury round=1
Small things can be ringed too -- the balance, the date, a single view tab.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=view.army
The bottom bar is eight views of the same map. This one is the army.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=panel.country
And whole panels, when the point is the panel rather than a control in it.
---
@Signals Officer
A deliberate mistake follows: the next page points at a name nothing offers,
and the log says so with the list of what it could have meant.
---
@Signals Officer
:: point=tab.ecnomy
Nothing is ringed, because "tab.ecnomy" is not a thing.
---
@Signals Officer
And now nothing is ringed, and the map is yours again.
